  "headline_sentences": [
    "Leave-one-family-out (6 rare UDP DDoS families, one retrain each): forced to answer, the model passes 70.5% of a never-seen family's flows as benign. With the reject option at 0.99 that falls to 7.7%, because 91.4% go to an analyst as unknown. The worst family, UDP-bypass-v1, still passes 15.2% as benign (95% CI 11.0% to 20.7%).",
    "That is not novelty detection. In the same rounds, the rare families that WERE in training are passed as benign 59.2% of the time with no knob and 7.0% at 0.99, where 84.9% of them go to an analyst too: the lift over them is 0.065. The knob protects against a new flood by rejecting rare floods in general.",
    "Held-out split (6,570 flows, stratified test mix): wrong automatic verdicts fall from 1,683 to 44 per 10,000 flows (1,106 to 29) at 0.99, with 35.2% of flows sent to an analyst. That share is 59.1% for benign flows, so a network with more benign traffic than this capped sample would send more, not less."
  ],
  "resume_sentences": [
    "Measured open-set behaviour with leave-one-family-out retrains over 6 rare UDP DDoS families plus a rarity control: forced to answer, the model passes 70.5% of a never-seen family as benign; a 0.99 reject option cuts that to 7.7% by routing 91.4% to an analyst, and the control shows trained-on rare families routed almost as often (84.9%), so the gain comes from rejecting rare floods, not from detecting novelty.",
    "On a stratified held-out split of 6,570 flows, a 0.99 reject threshold cut wrong automatic verdicts from 1,683 to 44 per 10,000 flows by sending 35.2% of flows to an analyst, 59.1% of benign ones."
  ]
}
